  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1231 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nIt is a beautiful fall day, thanks to the weak synoptic descent\namidst northwest flow aloft, with a digging trough over the \nGreat Lakes region and a weak ridge building over the Rocky \nMountains. Clear skies, aside from some high- level cirrus \nclouds potentially drifting into the northern potions of the \narea, and modest dew points in the upper 30s to low 40s will \nallow high temperatures to climb into the upper 60s to low 70s \nthis afternoon. Calm winds will gradually become more southerly \nthis afternoon and evening as a high pressure center in the \nSoutheast continues to dictate the low-level flow.\n\nFog is again possible tonight into tomorrow morning, as weak \nwinds overnight combine with efficient radiative cooling. Recent\nREFS guidance currently depicts a 30-60% chance of visibilities\ndropping below 1 mile tomorrow morning, with this fog potential\nmainly confined to low-lying areas along and south of Highway \n136. Low temperatures are expected to fall into the upper 30s to\nlow 40s by tomorrow morning. \n\nThe warm, dry weather is expected to continue through the start\nof the work week, as the flow aloft remains west-northwesterly.\nWinds will generally be out of the west to northwest tomorrow \nafternoon, helping support high temperatures peaking into the \nupper 60s to low 70s. Fog is again possible (roughly 40-70% \nchance) tomorrow night into Tuesday morning. However, its \ndevelopment will be hindered by the westerly surface flow, \npotentially reducing dew points low enough to not reach \nsaturation despite the efficient radiation cooling under the \nclear skies (similar to what happened last night). A shortwave \ntrough will pass over the region Monday evening through Tuesday,\nsupporting an area of low pressure to the east of the area. \nWhile this wave is not expected to bring precipitation to the \narea, it will bring high temperatures back into the 60s Tuesday \nand Wednesday. Wednesday will see gusty northerly winds in the \nwake of a cold front Tuesday night.\n\nOverall, models are in general agreement that a ridge will \ndevelop over the Intermountain West and a trough feature will \nreside over the Midwest/Ohio Valley. However, there is still \nsome disagreement in there these features set up, causing a \nlarge temperature distribution in both the NBM and LREF. The \nGEFS (and parent GFS recent run) still place this trough \nslightly more to the west, with more shortwaves embedded within \nthis flow, supporting the cooler end of the range (highs in the \nlow 50s). On the contrary, the ENS (and the most recent run of \nits parent ECMWF model) continue to show this trough moving \nthrough slightly quicker, placing it further to the East. This \neastward placement would allow the warmer end of the spectrum \n(highs in the upper 50s).",
